From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 15:02:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Add a js-quickjs feature for running on the QuickJS
 mozjs shim

With the js-quickjs feature, Servo runs on servo-quickjs
(https://github.com/openharmony-rs/servo-quickjs), which implements the
mozjs API on QuickJS. The feature builds only with mozjs patched to the
shim, e.g. in .cargo/config.toml:

    [patch.crates-io]
    mozjs = { git = "https://github.com/openharmony-rs/servo-quickjs", rev = "..." }

QuickJS frees objects by reference counting and collects cycles, so
under js-quickjs:
- Dom<T> and DomRoot<T> hold a reference count on their target, also
  when the target's reflector is set later (a DissimilarOriginWindow's
  WindowProxy), and roots go through no root list;
- the reflector of a DOM object holds none on its own object, and is
  neither traced nor released by the Heap drop barrier; the reflector of
  a TracedPromise holds a counted, traced one;
- finalizers drop the Dom<T> fields of an object while the collector
  knows which objects it frees, so WindowProxy finalizes through
  finalize_common;
- live Trusted references are counted until swept, and the shim's
  autorelease pool is drained between tasks, on the script thread and
  on worker threads;
- reflectors are initialized before they are rooted, DOM objects are
  left alone once the runtime is destroyed, and the Debugger API is
  left out.

Under SpiderMonkey, WindowProxy's finalizer does what it did before, and
MutDom and MutNullableDom no longer copy Dom<T> bitwise, which is
equivalent.

Fork note: experimental, to evaluate QuickJS as the engine of a smaller
profile (servo-quickjs docs/design.md). All QuickJS code is behind the
feature, and the QuickJS workflow builds it and runs WPT with it. Not
meant for upstream in this form.

Rebasing: under js-quickjs a Dom<T> holds a count, so a bitwise copy of
one, as upstream's ptr::read in MutDom and MutNullableDom was, releases
it twice. This must find nothing; clone or borrow instead:

    git grep ptr::read components/script_bindings components/script

Removing such a copy can leave a crown unrooted_must_root expectation
unfulfilled, which fails the Linux CI build (-D warnings) but no local
build. Check with crown installed from the tree (cargo install --path
support/crown):

    RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach check --use-crown

Validation: On ohos-main 8fab1661b2c, before folding: servo-quickjs `just test`, `just test-verify` and `just test-asan` (79 tests each) and `just fuzz-smoke 60` pass; `./mach fmt` and `./mach test-tidy` are clean, and servoshell builds without warnings with and without js-quickjs. WPT with debug builds of the same commit, QuickJS with QJS_VERIFY_TRACING=1 against SpiderMonkey, `--processes 2`, html/semantics/scripting-1, html/webappapis/microtask-queuing, html/webappapis/scripting, import-maps, workers/modules, webidl and dom/events (1087 tests): no panics or verifier aborts; worse with QuickJS only the three dynamic-import/microtasks/basic variants, which time out in a debug build and pass with `--timeout-multiplier 4`; better on 3 tests. On af58e6041ff: release builds with both engines, the QuickJS one through the QuickJS workflow's patch step (servo-quickjs 0605066), and `./mach smoketest` passes with both. The worker sweep was added after these runs, from the ownership review: with it, 1000 XHRs in a worker no longer leave 1000 XMLHttpRequest objects alive (probe build of the spike). Full WPT on this stack, release builds with servo-quickjs ed76452, 58,101 tests, then 3 reruns per engine of every difference: outside the WebAssembly-dependent tests, QuickJS is consistently worse only in the three autoplay-*-permissions-policy tests, two WebGL conformance tests that time out on SpiderMonkey without JIT too, and single subtests of structured-cloning-error-stack and localeCompare (no Intl on desktop). After a review of these fixes, workers also release the pool after each batch, not only before the next one. With servo-quickjs 3286cd6, release build: `./mach smoketest` passes, and 2138 tests (cross-origin objects, WindowProxy, Location, history, windows, forms, structured cloning, WebIDL, DOM, fetch responses, workers) match the full run's QuickJS results, except one subtest that now passes and three tests that are flaky with SpiderMonkey too. Rebased onto 7f6279eb9b9 (the TimerListener and closed-pipeline leak fixes, and #48739, which this stack had carried as a backport): release builds with both engines and a checked-release QuickJS build pass the smoketest without warnings, and 2464 WPT tests (the 2138 above plus timers, iframes and cross-document navigation) match the full run's QuickJS results, except one test now passing, as on SpiderMonkey, and two customizable-select tests that are flaky on both engines (select-appearance-hover crashed once under load and fails 5 of 5 alone on both). `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach check --use-crown`, as the Linux CI job builds, passes: the crown expectation on `MutNullableDom::get_unrooted` went away with the `ptr::read` it covered.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; agent sessions wrote and tested the js-quickjs commits, and the agent folded them into this patch and added the worker sweep)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
